FURB118 [*] Use `operator.is_` instead of defining a lambda
 --> FURB118_basedpython.by:2:7
  |
1 | # `===` is the identity `operator.is_` implements
2 | is_ = lambda x, y: x === y  # FURB118
  |       ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
3 | is_not = lambda x, y: x !== y  # FURB118
  |
help: Replace with `operator.is_`
  |
1 | # `===` is the identity `operator.is_` implements
  - is_ = lambda x, y: x === y  # FURB118
2 + import operator
3 + is_ = operator.is_  # FURB118
4 | is_not = lambda x, y: x !== y  # FURB118
  |
note: This is an unsafe fix and may change runtime behavior

FURB118 [*] Use `operator.is_not` instead of defining a lambda
 --> FURB118_basedpython.by:3:10
  |
1 | # `===` is the identity `operator.is_` implements
2 | is_ = lambda x, y: x === y  # FURB118
3 | is_not = lambda x, y: x !== y  # FURB118
  |          ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
4 |
5 | # a type test asks about a type, which no `operator` function does
  |
help: Replace with `operator.is_not`
  |
1 | # `===` is the identity `operator.is_` implements
2 + import operator
3 | is_ = lambda x, y: x === y  # FURB118
  - is_not = lambda x, y: x !== y  # FURB118
4 + is_not = operator.is_not  # FURB118
5 |
  |
note: This is an unsafe fix and may change runtime behavior
